==English==

===Etymology===
Coined by [[w:William_Shakespeare|William Shakespeare]], in ''Anthony and Cleopatra'', act 1, scene 5:<ref>Jack, Albert (2005). ''Red herrings and white elephants: the origins of the phrases we use everyday.'' HarperCollins. page [http://books.google.co.jp/books?id=NSMq-PhYVq4C&pg=PA44 44]. {{ISBN|978-0-06-084337-3}}. “The phrase is a simple one with a simple origin provided, once again, by Shakespeare.”</ref>
: My '''salad days''', when I was green in judgment.

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-plural noun|head=[[salad]] [[day]]s}}

# A [[period]] of [[inexperienced]] [[youthful]] [[innocence]] accompanied by [[enthusiasm]] and [[idealism]].
#* {{RQ:Wodehouse Offing|chapter=XX|passage=“I'll bet he was swiping things as a small boy.” “Only biscuits.” “I beg your pardon?” “Or crackers you would call them, wouldn't you? He was telling me he occasionally pinched a cracker or two in his '''salad days'''.”}}
#* '''2015''' Sir Malcolm Murray, Penny Dreadful s2e6, 23m30s
#*: Do you know I've not been to a ball in ages? I used to be quite the dancer in my '''salad days''', if you can believe such a thing.

